@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+#!/bin/bash
+# The main display goes, and comes back, WITH ARBOR. The display at 0 0 is the main one, and every other display's numbers, and every window's, are counted from its corner. No
+# recording so far has a change of the main display in it: every display that came or went was another one. THE QUESTION: when the main display goes, and when it comes back, does a
+# window's own word of its move come before the news of the displays, by how many milliseconds, and in which list's numbers is the frame it reports? For each window and each change.
+# Part G of probe-went-and-came-back-journey.sh; probe-the-main-display-goes-control.sh plays it without Arbor.
+#
+# IT DECIDES SOMETHING, BY A RULE WRITTEN BEFORE ITS ANSWER. The product owner's words, before the run:
+#   "(a) at a change in which the MAIN display goes or comes, a window's word of a move arrives while macOS's own list of displays is STILL THE OLD ONE (before macOS's first word
+#   about its screens and before its list is new) AND THE FRAME IT REPORTS IS IN THE NEW LIST's NUMBERS: once or more in ten rounds. (b) never in ten rounds; early words that are all
+#   in the OLD list's numbers are (b) too, and what was seen is said. (c) a hosted Mac cannot stage it: as (b), 'never staged'."
+#   What follows from each: (a) a board row goes into the sentence that decides the next cut; (b) it stays wanted and holds nothing; (c) as (b), and the row says "never staged".
+#   Why the second half of (a): a word in the old list's numbers under the old list is true and harms nobody; a frame in the new numbers, read under the old list, is a false place.
+# The script itself judges nothing: it stages, writes down, and puts the times side by side.
+#
+#   scripts/mac-shell/run.sh --no-preflight "<why>" scripts/mac-shell/probe-the-main-display-goes.sh scripts/mac-shell/probe-the-main-display-goes-control.sh
+#
+# Either by itself or both in one dispatch, in either order: each builds what it needs and leaves the Mac as it found it, however it ends: once A is unplugged the Mac's own display is
+# the main one again, because it is the only one.
+# Why no pre-flight: the fake Mac has no stand-in for vdisplay, apptool or the sampler, so a rehearsal ends with exit code 2 before the first step.
+#
+# THE TIME, reckoned from a dry run against stand-ins about as slow as the tools, and from what the build, the apps and Arbor's start took in earlier playings: a change costs 6 to 7 s (four
+# of waiting: Arbor is told up to 2.7 s after macOS's first word; the rest is lines), a put 3 to 4 s. R1: 16 s. R3: 26 s, or 33 s if A comes back as the main display and H has to be
+# made it again: ten times, 260 to 330 s. R2: 16 s. Before them the build (15 to 40 s), the Mac, six windows, Arbor's start and A's first coming (55 s); after them 20 s.
+# SIX AND A QUARTER TO EIGHT MINUTES; with the control, ten and a half to thirteen. No round begins after 800 s (JOURNEY_BUDGET), and R2 comes last; lib.sh ends the script at 1,020 s.
+#
+# WHAT IT PLAYS. H is the Mac's own display, 1600×900 if it offers that (`H, the Mac's own display …` says what it is); A a virtual one, 1920×1080, at its right. Arbor runs as a person
+# runs it, all but its welcome window: its trace on, nothing docked, the settings it ships with.
+#   TEXT (TextEdit), LIVES (Notes)          wholly on A, the display that goes
+#   WIDE (TextEdit, a second document)      wholly on A and 1,700 points wide: wider than H
+#   STRADDLES (Terminal)                    across the edge between H and A, a little more than half of it on A
+#   DESK (Script Editor)                    wholly on H, the display that stays
+#   LITTLE (TextEdit, a third document)     800×600, left with little of it showing and its title bar within reach: it hangs over H's bottom edge at the border, 36 rows of it on H, 40
+#                                           columns on A, which reaches 180 points lower than H, the rest on no display: some 7 % shows. If macOS does not have it there: `!!!`, and on
+# Four real apps. No Java app: it would cost eight seconds and a seventh of every look. Before every round each window is put at its place through Accessibility, with its size, and seen
+# there by the window server (`put`, `placed`). No two have one size, and the two lists' numbers differ by H's width: no frame can be read as both.
+#   R1, once      the control, what the changes on record are: A is NOT the main display. It goes (-goes: it is unplugged) and comes (-comes: it is plugged in again)
+#   R3, TEN TIMES the question: A is made the main display (-A-main), the windows are put again, A GOES, and COMES, and is left where it comes (`came-back`: as the main display?)
+#   R2, once      the numbers change and nothing goes: A is made the main display (-A-main); then H again (-H-main)
+# `staged: yes (<round>: …)` or `staged: no: <the tools' words> (<round>)`, ONCE IN EVERY ROUND of R3 and R2: whether A was the main display, by `vdisplay list`, just before it was
+# unplugged. Outcome (c) is ten times `staged: no`. A display is made the main one by `screens main <id>`, which moves every display by the same distance in one configuration, so that
+# the arrangement stays and is counted from another corner, as after the menu bar has been dragged to another display in System Settings; failing that, or with
+# JOURNEY_MAIN_BY=move, by `vdisplay move <id> 0 0`, which leaves it to macOS where the other display goes. NOBODY HAS TRIED EITHER ON A HOSTED MAC. If both are refused twice they are
+# not tried again; R1 is played, and the script ends with exit code 0.
+#
+# THE NEWS OF THE DISPLAYS HAS THREE MEANINGS, and a window's word three witnesses, all on one clock:
+#   (i)   macOS's own first word about its screens      screens.log: N (didChangeScreenParameters), R (the display reconfiguration callback). The trace: the raw line didChangeScreenParameters
+#   (ii)  the moment macOS's list of displays is new    screens.log: C (CoreGraphics' list), X (AppKit's), asked for every 4 ms. The trace: its `screens` line, and its reads of the displays.
+#                                                       samples.log: D, V, Y, four times a second
+#   (iii) the moment Arbor is told                      the trace: the event `displaysChanged`
+#   a window's word       screens.log: H, with both lists AS THE SAME PROCESS READ THEM WHEN THE WORD ARRIVED. macos-reports.log: AXMoved, AXResized. The trace: the event `changed`, and the
+#                         read of the displays that follows it
+# apptool watch, which writes macos-reports.log, hears neither NSApplicationDidChangeScreenParameters nor CGDisplayReconfiguration, and it has no NSApplication to hear the first with:
+# that is more than a few lines in a tool every script needs. Hence screens.swift, new with this probe, which hears the notices, the lists and the windows in ONE process on one thread,
+# as Arbor does. NO MAC HAS COMPILED IT YET. If it does not compile the script says so (`!!!`) and goes on: screens.log then has C lines alone, from a loop around `vdisplay list`,
+# some twenty times a second, and (i) can be read from the trace only.
+#
+# HOW TO READ IT. Every line of the output is explained at part G in the journey, the last ones in words-before-the-news.py. The steps are a-<round>-<put|A-main|put-again|goes|comes|H-main>.
+#   ONE CLOCK, the Mac's own, to the millisecond: samples.log and screens.log count it in milliseconds since 1970, macos-reports.log and Arbor's log write it as UTC. A trace counts
+#   seconds from its own beginning: `trace-zero <trace> between <ms> and <ms>` says when that was. THE MARKER of a change is the moment the command that makes it was given:
+#   `changed <step> at <ms>` in the output, `M change <step>` in samples.log (after `M begin <n> display-<step>`), `SCRIPT == <n> display-<step>` a second earlier in macos-reports.log.
+#   - `word <round> <goes|comes|A-main|H-main> <NAME> <id> first-word <ms|none> list-new <ms|unknown> notice <ms|unknown> told <ms|no> | frame <x y w h> numbers <…> list-at-word <old|new|unknown>`,
+#     at the very end, for every window and change, in milliseconds after the marker: by screens.log. `word-reports`: the word by macos-reports.log. `word-trace`: all four by the trace.
+#     `early …`: EVERY word that came before the notice or under the old list. `tally …`: the counts, the wider readings among them (before Arbor was told; before the list was new, in
+#     whichever numbers). They are made from the logs by words-before-the-news.py, which can be run again on the folder that came back: check it against the logs before believing it.
+#   - IN WHICH LIST's NUMBERS? `expects <round> <change> <NAME> <id> old <x y w h> on <display> same-glass-in-new <x y w h>`, before each change: the frame now, and that same place on the
+#     glass in the new list's numbers. `arrangement <step> … | shift <dx> <dy>` and the whole `vdisplay list`, after it: the offset that really applied. A window that macOS also moves
+#     on the glass fits neither: `main <step> <NAME> … | accessibility … | server … is … | shown … on …`, 0.5 and 3.5 s after the marker, has where it rests, in the new numbers;
+#     `numbers resting-new` and `resting-old` in the `word` lines are that place in either list's numbers.
+#   - Where is LITTLE after the main display went, and after it came back? `little <round> LITTLE <id> before <x y w h> after-it-went <x y w h> after-it-came-back <x y w h> same-as-before
+#     <yes|no> | before, in the numbers of the list now: <x y w h>`, after -comes in R1 and R3; each frame in the numbers of the list in force at that moment. The control's are macOS alone.
+#   - What does Arbor do? `arbor-set <step> <NAME> <id> <before|after>-told … asked … took …`; `told`, `heard`, `reported` beside it; `arbor <step> | …`, its own account. The traces come
+#     back whole: traces/*-went-and-came-back-G1.jsonl, -G2, …
+#   - samples.log has the window server's frames (W lines) as often as the sampler manages of the 240 times a second it is asked for: its last line says how often that was.
+#   A line that begins `!!!` spoils the reading, `SKIPPED:` names what was not played, `rounds played to the end: …` sums it up.
+#
+# WHAT IT CANNOT TELL. Whether the main display COMES is macOS's choice, not the script's: A is plugged in and left where it comes, and `came-back` says whether it came as the main
+# display; if it never does, only its going is staged, and -A-main is the one change in which the numbers turn the other way. A is virtual, and a hosted Mac's own display is no monitor either: no cable, lid, dock or sleep, and nobody drags the menu bar in System Settings. Each witness says
+# when a word reached THAT process: screens.log's is not an app with windows and not Arbor, and three processes listening may be served in another order than one. The frame in an H line
+# and in macos-reports.log is Accessibility's answer a moment after the word, not something the word carries. Six windows of four apps, ten rounds, one playing on one Mac.
+#
+# Only on GitHub's Macs: it plugs a display in and out, changes which display is the main one, switches the display's size, changes Reduce Motion and puts it back, hides apps, opens
+# and quits four apps.
+JOURNEY_ARBOR=yes
+ARBOR_RECORD=1
+JOURNEY_PARTS=G
+JOURNEY_REST=${JOURNEY_REST:-2}
+JOURNEY_BUDGET=${JOURNEY_BUDGET:-800}
+# As finely as the sampler goes.
+DAY_HZ=${DAY_HZ:-240}
+source "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/probe-went-and-came-back-journey.sh"
